      "title": "AR occlusion from a stereo depth map",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "DEPTH OCCLUSION - putting a virtual object BEHIND real geometry.\n\nTop row builds the scene's own z-buffer out of the stereo pair: calibrate from the CSVs, rectify, match (SGBM), fill the holes (RIC), reproject to XYZ, take channel 2, and NEGATE it.\n\nThat negation is not cosmetic. With this calibration stereoRectify returns a Q whose last row is negative, so reprojectImageTo3D hands back the negation of the camera frame - the median depth here is -11 m. Nothing downstream complains: a point cloud simply renders mirrored (which is what the 'Flip X' in the point-cloud exercises is for), and an occlusion test would just never fire. Always check the SIGN of a reprojected Z before trusting it.\n\nBottom row renders the same truck twice through the same calibrated camera. The left render has scene_depth unwired - the classic AR sticker, pasted over everything including the car in front of it. The right one has it wired, so the truck is cut wherever the photographed scene is nearer, and the 'occlusion' mask shows exactly which pixels were taken away.\n\nscene_depth_bias exists because stereo depth is NOISY, and the surface an object stands on is the one most likely to eat it: a positive bias pushes the scene back so the model wins ties. Depth error also grows with the square of distance - at f*baseline = 117 px*m one 1/16 px disparity step is 0.05 m at 10 m but 1.34 m at 50 m - so keep the object in the near field, where the depth is worth trusting.\n\nThe intrinsics come from decomposeProjectionMatrix(P1), NOT from the CSV: the background is the RECTIFIED frame and rectification moves K (fx 431.55 -> 433.77, cy 240.0 -> 241.94 here).\n\nTry: drag the truck with the viewer's gizmo (it writes object_transform, so the next run re-renders it there) and watch it pass behind and in front of the parked car; turn the viewer's 'occlusion' checkbox off to compare live."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
